Subject: [PATCH] Add a platform profile for the PCSpecialist / TongFang GM7MG7P

This driver has no platform-profile support at all, so this patch adds it: a
`profile` and `profile_cycles` sysfs pair, a `platform_profile` callback on
`struct uniwill_device_descriptor`, and a `UNIWILL_OSD_PERFORMANCE_MODE_TOGGLE`
arm that cycles instead of reporting KEY_F14 on boards that carry a handler.
The GM7MG7P descriptor is the first one to carry one.

The reason for writing it in this driver's own idiom rather than on top of
`dev_pm_platform_profile_register()` is a practical one and worth stating
before a reviewer finds it: the patch was written without a kernel headers
tree to compile against, so an API whose current signature could not be
checked here was not something to guess at. The enum carries the kernel's
member names in the kernel's order, so moving the attribute to
`dev_pm_platform_profile_register()` later is a translation of about twenty
lines and not a redesign. If you would rather have that in the same patch,
say so and it can be done -- it just needs to be built once.

The GM7MG7P handler:

  - maps the three real modes onto low-power/balanced/performance as
    Office/Gaming/Turbo, which is the order the OSD cycles them;
  - gates Turbo on EC_ADDR_BIOS_INFO_3 (0x049F) bit 1, and deliberately not on
    the existing FAN_TURBO_SUPPORTED, which is EC_ADDR_SUPPORT_5 (0x0742)
    bit 4: 0x0742 reads 0x02 on this board, so that bit is clear while Turbo
    works. I have not reconciled the two and do not think this patch should;
  - writes the power limits as well as the mode byte, out of the EC's own
    per-mode default blocks. This is the part that is easy to get wrong by
    omission: on this EC the mode byte alone does not move them. Holding
    EC_ADDR_MANUAL_FAN_CTRL at each of the three values and watching moved
    neither EC_ADDR_PL1/PL2/PL4_SETTING nor the fan curve, and none of the 29
    firmware sites for that byte touches another address. A handler that wrote
    only the mode byte would report a new profile while the machine stayed in
    the old one;
  - carries no wattage. The limits are read out of the EC and written back, so
    a board whose EC was programmed differently follows its own EC;
  - leaves the fan curve alone. The OEM service's curve handshake parks the EC
    with EC_ADDR_AP_OEM bit 0 clear, and that is the state in which the EC's own
    zeroing of the power limits runs. The driver holds that bit set from probe
    and nothing here clears it, which is what makes writing the limits without
    the curve safe -- and the reason not to add the curve later without
    rethinking this.

Two things this patch does not claim. The mode bits' effect on fan behaviour is
not established: the measurement above was near-idle, and the fan duty moved the
same amount under a no-op control write, so what changed there was thermally. A
profile switch here changes the power limits; whether it changes the curve is
untested and should be read as untested. And the patch has been neither compiled
nor loaded -- it was written against the source alone, on a machine with no
kernel headers tree, so treat the first build as the review step.

Base commit: 5a24248f6422a0b673a47cbfd65e19a98eb4c8a9
  ("Bump minimum supported kernel version to 7.2"), the rev
  linux/patches/BASE_COMMIT pins and the same rev the local nix build fetches.
  The blob this diff is against hashes to 7a2eeae, which is the same base
  index line linux/patches/uniwill-laptop-force_charge_limit.patch carries.

To apply:
    git fetch https://github.com/Wer-Wolf/uniwill-laptop 5a24248f6422a0b673a47cbfd65e19a98eb4c8a9
    git am linux/patches/gm7mg7p-power-profile/uniwill-acpi-profile-gm7mg7p.patch
  or, to check it without applying:
    git apply --check linux/patches/gm7mg7p-power-profile/uniwill-acpi-profile-gm7mg7p.patch

Every address, bit and value the handler uses is recorded row by row with its
evidence in profile-map.csv beside this file.

This patch adds a GM7MG7P descriptor and the GM7MG7P DMI row that points at
it, and neither exists in the tree it is diffed against. It supersedes an
earlier patch of mine for the same board and the same rev, which added that
descriptor -- the same eight feature bits, and no profile -- and that same DMI
row: so the two are alternatives, not a series, and this one is the one to
apply. Applied to one tree in either order the second one fails at
uniwill-acpi.c:2860, the descriptor hunk, because the descriptor and the row
are then already there. If the descriptor and row reach upstream first, drop
those two hunks from this patch and rebase the rest onto them.

Apart from what it adds, no existing descriptor, DMI row, feature bit or
keymap entry is modified -- which is a statement about this diff applied on its
own to the rev named above, and about nothing else. The 0xB0 arm falls through
to the arm below it on every board whose descriptor leaves the callback NULL,
so no other machine changes behaviour.

diff --git a/uniwill-acpi.c b/uniwill-acpi.c
--- a/uniwill-acpi.c
+++ b/uniwill-acpi.c
@@ -348,6 +348,30 @@
 #define PWM_MAX			200
 #define FAN_TABLE_LENGTH	16
 
+/*
+ * Addresses a platform profile needs that are not named above. They are kept
+ * together rather than spread through the address list because they only make
+ * sense next to each other: a profile selects a mode byte, and replays the
+ * power limits out of the per-mode default block for that mode.
+ *
+ * EC_ADDR_BIOS_INFO_3 bit 1 is the Turbo capability bit on the boards that
+ * expose a profile, which is deliberately not EC_ADDR_SUPPORT_5's
+ * FAN_TURBO_SUPPORTED above.
+ */
+#define EC_ADDR_BIOS_INFO_3		0x049F
+#define TURBO_MODE_SUPPORTED		BIT(1)
+
+#define EC_ADDR_GAMING_PL_DEFAULT	0x0730
+#define EC_ADDR_OFFICE_PL_DEFAULT	0x0734
+#define EC_ADDR_TURBO_PL_DEFAULT	0x07A7
+
+/*
+ * The mode byte minus Fan Boost. FAN_MODE_BOOST belongs to the OSD, which adds
+ * it while Fan Boost is on, so a profile must not clear it by writing the whole
+ * byte.
+ */
+#define FAN_PROFILE_MASK		(GENMASK(5, 0) | FAN_MODE_USER)
+
 #define LED_CHANNELS		3
 
 #define KBD_LED_CHANNELS	3
@@ -375,6 +399,33 @@
 	USB_C_POWER_PRIORITY_PERFORMANCE,
 };
 
+/*
+ * The platform profile vocabulary of the kernel's platform-profile interface,
+ * with the same member names and the same order, so that a board whose
+ * descriptor carries a platform_profile callback can be handed to
+ * dev_pm_platform_profile_register() without translating.
+ */
+enum uniwill_platform_profile {
+	UNIWILL_PLATFORM_PROFILE_UNKNOWN,
+	UNIWILL_PLATFORM_PROFILE_LOW_POWER,
+	UNIWILL_PLATFORM_PROFILE_BALANCED,
+	UNIWILL_PLATFORM_PROFILE_PERFORMANCE,
+	UNIWILL_PLATFORM_PROFILE_MAX,
+};
+
+static const char * const uniwill_profile_names[UNIWILL_PLATFORM_PROFILE_MAX] = {
+	[UNIWILL_PLATFORM_PROFILE_UNKNOWN] = "unknown",
+	[UNIWILL_PLATFORM_PROFILE_LOW_POWER] = "low-power",
+	[UNIWILL_PLATFORM_PROFILE_BALANCED] = "balanced",
+	[UNIWILL_PLATFORM_PROFILE_PERFORMANCE] = "performance",
+};
+
+static const u16 uniwill_pl_settings[] = {
+	EC_ADDR_PL1_SETTING,
+	EC_ADDR_PL2_SETTING,
+	EC_ADDR_PL4_SETTING,
+};
+
 struct uniwill_data {
 	struct device *dev;
 	acpi_handle handle;
@@ -418,6 +469,9 @@
 	struct notifier_block nb;
 	struct mutex usb_c_power_priority_lock; /* Protects dependent bit write and state safe */
 	enum usb_c_power_priority_options last_usb_c_power_priority_option;
+	struct mutex profile_lock;	/* Protects the platform profile and its registers */
+	enum uniwill_platform_profile current_profile;
+	bool turbo_supported;
 };
 
 struct uniwill_battery_entry {
@@ -432,6 +486,21 @@
 	u8 lightbar_max_brightness;
 	/* Executed during driver probing */
 	int (*probe)(struct uniwill_data *data);
+	/*
+	 * Optional. Boards that leave it NULL get no profile attribute and keep
+	 * reporting UNIWILL_OSD_PERFORMANCE_MODE_TOGGLE as KEY_F14, exactly as
+	 * they do today. Returns 0 on success and a negative errno on failure;
+	 * profile_store() hands that errno to userspace unchanged.
+	 */
+	int (*platform_profile)(struct uniwill_data *data,
+				enum uniwill_platform_profile profile);
+};
+
+struct uniwill_profile_entry {
+	/* The bytes this profile writes into EC_ADDR_MANUAL_FAN_CTRL */
+	u8 mode_byte;
+	/* First byte of the EC's per-mode power-limit default block */
+	u16 pl_defaults;
 };
 
 static bool force;
@@ -619,6 +687,10 @@
 	case EC_ADDR_CTGP_DB_TPP_OFFSET:
 	case EC_ADDR_CTGP_DB_DB_OFFSET:
 	case EC_ADDR_USB_C_POWER_PRIORITY:
+	case EC_ADDR_MANUAL_FAN_CTRL:
+	case EC_ADDR_PL1_SETTING:
+	case EC_ADDR_PL2_SETTING:
+	case EC_ADDR_PL4_SETTING:
 		return true;
 	default:
 		return false;
@@ -665,6 +737,14 @@
 	case EC_ADDR_CTGP_DB_TPP_OFFSET:
 	case EC_ADDR_CTGP_DB_DB_OFFSET:
 	case EC_ADDR_USB_C_POWER_PRIORITY:
+	case EC_ADDR_BIOS_INFO_3:
+	case EC_ADDR_GAMING_PL_DEFAULT:
+	case EC_ADDR_OFFICE_PL_DEFAULT:
+	case EC_ADDR_TURBO_PL_DEFAULT:
+	case EC_ADDR_MANUAL_FAN_CTRL:
+	case EC_ADDR_PL1_SETTING:
+	case EC_ADDR_PL2_SETTING:
+	case EC_ADDR_PL4_SETTING:
 		return true;
 	default:
 		return false;
@@ -691,6 +771,17 @@
 	case EC_ADDR_OEM_4:
 	case EC_ADDR_CHARGE_CTRL:
 	case EC_ADDR_USB_C_POWER_PRIORITY:
+	/*
+	 * The EC rewrites the mode byte on its own -- on a boot-time default
+	 * mode, on its own Turbo path, and whenever the Fan Boost arms decide to
+	 * clear the bit -- and it zeroes the power limits on a path that is not
+	 * gated on the mode. A cached read of any of these would report the
+	 * driver's own last write back to it instead of what the EC believes.
+	 */
+	case EC_ADDR_MANUAL_FAN_CTRL:
+	case EC_ADDR_PL1_SETTING:
+	case EC_ADDR_PL2_SETTING:
+	case EC_ADDR_PL4_SETTING:
 		return true;
 	default:
 		return false;
@@ -1250,6 +1341,70 @@
 
 static DEVICE_ATTR_RW(usb_powershare_high);
 
+static ssize_t profile_store(struct device *dev, struct device_attribute *attr,
+			     const char *buf, size_t count)
+{
+	struct uniwill_data *data = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
+	enum uniwill_platform_profile profile;
+	int ret;
+
+	ret = sysfs_match_string(uniwill_profile_names, buf);
+	if (ret < 0)
+		return ret;
+
+	/* What the machine is in right now is not something to ask it to be in. */
+	if (!ret)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	profile = ret;
+	if (profile == UNIWILL_PLATFORM_PROFILE_PERFORMANCE && !data->turbo_supported)
+		return -EOPNOTSUPP;
+
+	ret = device_descriptor.platform_profile(data, profile);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	return count;
+}
+
+static ssize_t profile_show(struct device *dev, struct device_attribute *attr, char *buf)
+{
+	struct uniwill_data *data = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
+	enum uniwill_platform_profile profile;
+
+	mutex_lock(&data->profile_lock);
+	profile = data->current_profile;
+	mutex_unlock(&data->profile_lock);
+
+	return sysfs_emit(buf, "%s\n", uniwill_profile_names[profile]);
+}
+
+static DEVICE_ATTR_RW(profile);
+
+static ssize_t profile_cycles_show(struct device *dev, struct device_attribute *attr,
+				   char *buf)
+{
+	struct uniwill_data *data = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
+	int i, len = 0;
+
+	guard(mutex)(&data->profile_lock);
+
+	/* UNKNOWN is what the machine might be in, not something to ask it to
+	 * be in, so the list starts where profile_store() starts accepting. */
+	for (i = UNIWILL_PLATFORM_PROFILE_LOW_POWER;
+	     i < UNIWILL_PLATFORM_PROFILE_MAX; i++) {
+		if (i == UNIWILL_PLATFORM_PROFILE_PERFORMANCE && !data->turbo_supported)
+			continue;
+
+		len += sysfs_emit_at(buf, len, "%s%s", len ? " " : "",
+				     uniwill_profile_names[i]);
+	}
+
+	return len;
+}
+
+static DEVICE_ATTR_RO(profile_cycles);
+
 static struct attribute *uniwill_attrs[] = {
 	/* Keyboard-related */
 	&dev_attr_fn_lock.attr,
@@ -1263,6 +1411,9 @@
 	&dev_attr_usb_c_power_priority.attr,
 	&dev_attr_ac_auto_boot.attr,
 	&dev_attr_usb_powershare_high.attr,
+	/* Platform-profile-related */
+	&dev_attr_profile.attr,
+	&dev_attr_profile_cycles.attr,
 	NULL
 };
 
@@ -1312,6 +1463,11 @@
 			return attr->mode;
 	}
 
+	if (attr == &dev_attr_profile.attr || attr == &dev_attr_profile_cycles.attr) {
+		if (device_descriptor.platform_profile)
+			return attr->mode;
+	}
+
 	return 0;
 }
 
@@ -2168,6 +2324,36 @@
 	return devm_battery_hook_register(data->dev, &data->hook);
 }
 
+/*
+ * The next profile after the current one, in the order the OSD cycles them and
+ * wrapping round at the end. The target is chosen under profile_lock and then
+ * applied by the descriptor's callback, which takes the lock again; two racing
+ * callers therefore write the same profile rather than interleaving.
+ */
+static int uniwill_profile_cycle(struct uniwill_data *data)
+{
+	enum uniwill_platform_profile profile;
+	int i;
+
+	mutex_lock(&data->profile_lock);
+
+	for (i = data->current_profile + 1; i < UNIWILL_PLATFORM_PROFILE_MAX; i++) {
+		if (i == UNIWILL_PLATFORM_PROFILE_PERFORMANCE && !data->turbo_supported)
+			continue;
+
+		break;
+	}
+
+	if (i == UNIWILL_PLATFORM_PROFILE_MAX)
+		profile = UNIWILL_PLATFORM_PROFILE_LOW_POWER;
+	else
+		profile = i;
+
+	mutex_unlock(&data->profile_lock);
+
+	return device_descriptor.platform_profile(data, profile);
+}
+
 static int uniwill_notifier_call(struct notifier_block *nb, unsigned long action, void *dummy)
 {
 	struct uniwill_data *data = container_of(nb, struct uniwill_data, nb);
@@ -2239,6 +2425,16 @@
 			return NOTIFY_DONE;
 
 		return notifier_from_errno(uniwill_notify_kbd_led(data, 4));
+	case UNIWILL_OSD_PERFORMANCE_MODE_TOGGLE:
+		/*
+		 * Only a board whose descriptor carries a profile handler consumes
+		 * this here. Every other board falls through to the arm below and
+		 * still reports it as KEY_F14, so no existing machine changes.
+		 */
+		if (device_descriptor.platform_profile)
+			return notifier_from_errno(uniwill_profile_cycle(data));
+
+		fallthrough;
 	default:
 		mutex_lock(&data->input_lock);
 		sparse_keymap_report_event(data->input_device, action, 1, true);
@@ -2248,6 +2444,14 @@
 	}
 }
 
+static int uniwill_profile_init(struct uniwill_data *data)
+{
+	if (!device_descriptor.platform_profile)
+		return 0;
+
+	return devm_mutex_init(data->dev, &data->profile_lock);
+}
+
 static int uniwill_input_init(struct uniwill_data *data)
 {
 	int ret;
@@ -2375,6 +2579,10 @@
 	if (ret < 0)
 		return ret;
 
+	ret = uniwill_profile_init(data);
+	if (ret < 0)
+		return ret;
+
 	return uniwill_input_init(data);
 }
 
@@ -2860,6 +3068,142 @@
 	.kbd_led_max_brightness = 2,
 };
 
+/*
+ * The GM7MG7P's three real power modes, in the order the OSD cycles them.
+ *
+ * The mode bytes are the firmware's own encoding rather than this driver's: the
+ * OEM service writes them from SetFanMode, and the EC produces the same 0x10 on
+ * its own Turbo path. They are spelled here as FAN_MODE_USER | FAN_MODE_HIGH
+ * and FAN_MODE_TURBO because those are the names the byte already has in this
+ * file; Gaming is zero.
+ *
+ * pl_defaults is the first byte of the EC's per-mode PL1/PL2/PL4 default block.
+ * Each block is contiguous for three bytes and so are EC_ADDR_PL1/PL2/PL4_SETTING,
+ * so both halves of the replay are one bulk transfer each. No wattage appears
+ * anywhere in this file: the limits are read out of the EC at apply time, so a
+ * board whose EC was programmed with different limits follows its own EC rather
+ * than a constant.
+ */
+static const struct uniwill_profile_entry
+gm7mg7p_profiles[UNIWILL_PLATFORM_PROFILE_MAX] = {
+	[UNIWILL_PLATFORM_PROFILE_LOW_POWER] = {
+		.mode_byte = FAN_MODE_USER | FAN_MODE_HIGH,
+		.pl_defaults = EC_ADDR_OFFICE_PL_DEFAULT,
+	},
+	[UNIWILL_PLATFORM_PROFILE_BALANCED] = {
+		.mode_byte = 0,
+		.pl_defaults = EC_ADDR_GAMING_PL_DEFAULT,
+	},
+	[UNIWILL_PLATFORM_PROFILE_PERFORMANCE] = {
+		.mode_byte = FAN_MODE_TURBO,
+		.pl_defaults = EC_ADDR_TURBO_PL_DEFAULT,
+	},
+};
+
+static int gm7mg7p_platform_profile(struct uniwill_data *data,
+				    enum uniwill_platform_profile profile)
+{
+	struct acpi_battery_info bat = { 0 };
+	const struct uniwill_profile_entry *entry;
+	u8 limits[ARRAY_SIZE(uniwill_pl_settings)];
+	int ret;
+
+	if (profile >= ARRAY_SIZE(gm7mg7p_profiles))
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	entry = &gm7mg7p_profiles[profile];
+	if (!entry->pl_defaults)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	guard(mutex)(&data->profile_lock);
+
+	ret = regmap_bulk_read(data->regmap, entry->pl_defaults, limits, sizeof(limits));
+	if (ret < 0)
+		return ret;
+
+	/*
+	 * The mode byte on its own moves nothing else on this EC. Measured on
+	 * the machine: holding EC_ADDR_MANUAL_FAN_CTRL at each of the three
+	 * values moved neither the power limits nor the fan curve, and none of
+	 * the 29 firmware sites for this byte touches another address. The
+	 * limits are written here because nothing else writes them.
+	 */
+	ret = regmap_update_bits(data->regmap, EC_ADDR_MANUAL_FAN_CTRL,
+				 FAN_PROFILE_MASK, entry->mode_byte);
+	if (ret < 0)
+		return ret;
+
+	/*
+	 * On battery the OEM service writes zero to all three limits, and what
+	 * zero means to this EC is untested, so the limits are left alone off AC
+	 * rather than shipped as a guess.
+	 */
+	ret = acpi_get_battery_info(&bat);
+	if (ret < 0)
+		return ret;
+
+	if (bat.state != ACPI_BATT_STATUS_DISCHARGING) {
+		ret = regmap_bulk_write(data->regmap, EC_ADDR_PL1_SETTING, limits,
+					sizeof(limits));
+		if (ret < 0)
+			return ret;
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * Writing the limits at all is safe here only because uniwill_ec_init()
+	 * set ENABLE_MANUAL_CTRL, and the EC's own zeroing of these bytes runs
+	 * on the arm where that bit is clear. Nothing in this path clears it,
+	 * which is why the fan curve is not replayed along with them: the
+	 * curve's handshake needs the EC parked in exactly the state this
+	 * profile deliberately does not put it in.
+	 */
+	data->current_profile = profile;
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static int gm7mg7p_probe(struct uniwill_data *data)
+{
+	unsigned int value;
+	int ret;
+
+	/*
+	 * Turbo is offered on EC_ADDR_BIOS_INFO_3 bit 1 and not on the
+	 * FAN_TURBO_SUPPORTED bit of EC_ADDR_SUPPORT_5, which reads clear on
+	 * this board while Turbo works. The two have not been reconciled.
+	 */
+	ret = regmap_read(data->regmap, EC_ADDR_BIOS_INFO_3, &value);
+	if (ret < 0)
+		return ret;
+
+	data->turbo_supported = !!(value & TURBO_MODE_SUPPORTED);
+
+	/* EC_ADDR_BIOS_OEM_2 bit 4 clear is Office, set is Gaming. */
+	ret = regmap_read(data->regmap, EC_ADDR_BIOS_OEM_2, &value);
+	if (ret < 0)
+		return ret;
+
+	if (value & DEFAULT_MODE)
+		data->current_profile = UNIWILL_PLATFORM_PROFILE_BALANCED;
+	else
+		data->current_profile = UNIWILL_PLATFORM_PROFILE_LOW_POWER;
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static struct uniwill_device_descriptor gm7mg7p_descriptor __initdata = {
+	.features = UNIWILL_FEATURE_FN_LOCK |
+		    UNIWILL_FEATURE_SUPER_KEY |
+		    UNIWILL_FEATURE_BATTERY_CHARGE_MODES |
+		    UNIWILL_FEATURE_CPU_TEMP |
+		    UNIWILL_FEATURE_GPU_TEMP |
+		    UNIWILL_FEATURE_PRIMARY_FAN |
+		    UNIWILL_FEATURE_SECONDARY_FAN |
+		    UNIWILL_FEATURE_NVIDIA_CTGP_CONTROL,
+	.probe = gm7mg7p_probe,
+	.platform_profile = gm7mg7p_platform_profile,
+};
+
 static const struct dmi_system_id uniwill_dmi_table[] __initconst = {
 	{
 		.ident = "AiStone X4SP4NAL",
@@ -3316,6 +3660,14 @@
 		},
 		.driver_data = &tux_featureset_1_descriptor,
 	},
+	{
+		.ident = "PCSpecialist GM7MG7P",
+		.matches = {
+			DMI_MATCH(DMI_SYS_VENDOR, "PCSpecialist"),
+			DMI_EXACT_MATCH(DMI_BOARD_NAME, "GM7MG7P"),
+		},
+		.driver_data = &gm7mg7p_descriptor,
+	},
 	{ }
 };
 MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE(dmi, uniwill_dmi_table);
